The `ArrayUtils.addAll` method adds all the elements of the given arrays into a new array. The new array contains all the elements of `array1` followed by all the elements of `array2`. When an array is returned, it is always a new array. The type of the new array is determined based on the type of the first array unless the first array is null, in which case the type is the same as the second array.

Now, based on the information gathered, let's proceed with fault localization to identify the root cause of the issue.
The failure and the current bug report indicate that `ArrayUtils.addAll` does not handle mixed array types well. Looking at the code snippet of the method, it creates a new array with the component type of the first array (`array1`) and then copies the elements from both input arrays into the new array using `System.arraycopy`.

The root cause of the issue is that `System.arraycopy` does not perform type checking when copying elements from `array2` to the new array. This results in a situation where incompatible types can be stored in the new array without throwing an exception immediately.

To fix this issue, a type compatibility check should be performed when copying elements from `array2` to ensure that the types are compatible with the component type of the new array.

Proposed fix:
```java
2958 :         final Class<?> type1 = array1.getClass().getComponentType();
2959 :         final Class<?> type2 = array2.getClass().getComponentType();
2960 :         if (!type1.isAssignableFrom(type2)) {
2961 :             throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot add elements of incompatible types");
2962 :         }
```

This addition to the `ArrayUtils.addAll` method will ensure that elements of incompatible types cannot be added to the new array, addressing the reported issue.

